# Each phrase vouches that a choice was deliberate without giving the
# reason, so the fix gives the reason (`the pin is unused by design`
# becomes `the pin is unused, because the board pulls it high`) or drops
# the phrase.
#
# `by design` counts only at the end of a clause or after a form of
# `is`, because before a noun it names a team or a field (`grouped by
# design system`, `triaged by design leads`). `reviewed by design,
# engineering, and product` still fires, because the comma is a clause
# end. The clause-end list sits glued to `design`, the way the
# reassurance rule writes it.
expr main [(this is deliberate), (this is intentional), (by design[., ;, :, (,)]), ([is, was] by design)]
let message "Unexplained choice. Give the reason for the choice, or delete the phrase."
let description "Flags phrases that vouch a choice was deliberate without giving the reason."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Close releases the file handle."
allows "The pieces are grouped by design system, not by team."
allows "Issues are triaged by design leads every Monday."
